//! check: fail(E0101)
//! phase: none
//! conforms: gram.lex.char
//!
//! Issue #189: `[gram.lex.char]`'s prose said `\u{…}` takes one to six
//! hex digits while `CHAR_ESC` wrote `HEX_DIGIT+`, and nothing said
//! which text was normative. Measured: the lexer bounds the escape at
//! six, so the PROSE was the letter and the production amended
//! (`UNI_ESC`). The bound is on the escape's SHAPE, not on the value it
//! names — leading zeros count, so seven digits is refused here before
//! anything asks whether `0x0000041` is a scalar (it is: `'A'`).
//!
//! Its in-bounds twins already run in `strings/char_battery.lu`, which
//! spells the same values two, three, four, five and six digits wide.
//! The code is the string escape set's, not the char literal's
//! malformed-shape one: the digit count is a rule of
//! `[gram.lex.str.escape]`, which the char literal inherits, and it
//! binds inside `"…"` identically. Exactly one report fires here.

fn main() -> !int {
    let a = '\u{0000041}'
    if a == 'A' { 0 } else { 1 }
}
